Avoiding the repugnant conclusion by valuing variety
2023-09-12

There's a conclusion in population ethics called the repugnant conclusion. It's
described in some detail in [this
article](https://plato.stanford.edu/ARCHIVES/WIN2009/entries/repugnant-conclusion/#AppOthVal)
- give that a read if you're interested in some analysis by actual philosophers
or whatever.

Essentially, the problem is that if you value a population by adding the value
of individuals then for any population, there's a much larger population that
we value the same, where everyone has lives barely worth living.

One guy has 10 happiness, that's worth the same as 10 guys with 1 happiness,
that kind of thing. That's the repugnant conclusion. It's "repugnant" because
replacing very happy people with many much less happy people seems like it
should be worse.

I don't have this problem because I don't value populations by assigning
everyone a number and adding them together. Populations can only be valued as a
whole, and there are ways of valuing populations that avoid this conclusion.

<!--more-->

## How interesting is the world?

Our goal is to come up with a way to compare populations and decide which is
better. Let's start with one principle.

I think populations are better if they're more varied. Two Einsteins on two
islands each inventing general relativity isn't as good as one Einstein and one
Heisenberg each doing different things.

But we can't determine the value of a person just from how they are at a
specific time, we need to value their whole life.

What are the ways in which a life can be different? Let's say we have $n$
measures on which a life can vary ($n$ may be very large), so a person's life
$v$ can be represented as a vector $v \in \mathbb{R}^n$.

For a population $V$ (a set of lives), let's define $f(V) = \sum_{v,w \in V} |v - w|$,
i.e. the sum of the differences (distances) between lives lived by those in that population.

Here are some facts that follow from this value function:

* A small, varied population can be more valuable than a large, homogeneous
  population.

* People whose lives nearly match on these measures we picked don't add anything to
  the total value of the population.

And we avoid the repugnant conclusion.

## Avoiding the repugnant conclusion

Let's suppose we have our small but very valuable population. Can we replace it
by a larger but individually worse population?

The question barely makes sense because we don't assign value to individuals in
isolation anymore, but only to the population as a whole.

But if we replace a billion super varied lives with *any number* of subsistence
farmers barely getting by, the value we assign doesn't increase, because all of
those lives barely worth living are kind of samey and boring.

I think that makes sense: if we say all suffering is interchangeable and just
varies in intensity, lives with lots of suffering are substantially similar.
Suffering is just boring

## Even more repugnant conclusions

We trade the original repugnant conclusion for some even worse ones:

* Torturing two people in varied ways is better than torturing two people in
  the same way, even if the varied ways are more painful and worse by any
  reasonable morality. We can kind of get around this by only counting
  non-suffering people, but then we value one torture victim the same as a
  million, even though we "should" only prefer one victim.

* Two very different people can be more valuable than billions upon billions of
  the same person. I actually think this is reasonable in most cases, but we
  can get around this problem by bounding the possible differences between
  people. That's reasonable because a person can be 6 feet tall but not 6 light
  years tall - there can only be so much difference.

* Two populations valued separately may score low, but together score high. I
  don't think this is a problem because we're not valuing sub populations, only
  the whole population. Values of sub populations implies nothing and has no
  relation to the value of the whole population, except that making the
  population larger won't make the value smaller.

## What does this look like in practice?

Someone who values maximising total difference between people may do things
like:

* Advocate for people to live in different and extreme places e.g. on other
  planets or in space.

* Maintain differences between populations rather than allowing them to mix and
  completely homogenize.

* Desire some level of mixing between populations insofar as this creates new,
  different experiences.

* Preserve unique local customs, languages, and foods.

* Preserve unique local ecosystems and biodiversity.

* Advocate for more competition and variety of ideas.

Those are all good. Here are some bad things that hypothetical person might try:

* Increase the variety in a few people's lifestyles at the cost of the
  suffering of others.

* Not care about the suffering of others all that much as long as it makes for
  interesting reading.

* Actively cause suffering for entertainment or pleasure, as long as it's
  interesting.

Yeah, not great. Although I do cause suffering for my own pleasure on a regular
basis - flying long distances in a plane causes suffering through intense
carbon emissions. Eating meat also causes suffering.

## Conclusion

Don't ask me for advice.
